  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 308 PM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nTuesday - Friday...\n\nHigh pressure will shift east of the region and southerly flow will \nreturn. This will lead to increasing temperatures, with highs in the \nlow-mid 80s. Ridging aloft will be the main feature through Tuesday. \n\nOn Wednesday, a northern stream shortwave will begin to swing \nthrough the Great Lakes and the southern stream remnants of \nHurricane Polo will develop a low pressure system over the central \nPlains. Guidance continues to trend with these two systems moving \nseparately, with the northern system pushing through by Thursday. \nThe front associated with the northern system will stall at the \nsouthern system works northeast along the remnant boundary. Given \nthese features, precip chances will increase Wednesday night to \nslight chances over the northern half of the region. The cold front \nassociated with the southern stream system will move into the region \non Thursday/Friday and stall as the upper flow weakens. Looking into \nensembles, looks like we could see between 0.5-2.0 inches of \nrainfall Wednesday through Friday night. \n\n\nExtended Forecast Discussion...\n\nMoving into late next week, the slow moving upper level low looks to \nopen up and eventually head eastward.  Strong southwest flow may \nbring another round of warm air just ahead of the feature.  Current \nensemble guidance suggests that unsettled weather will likely be \nseen across the region next weekend.  As that system heads east, a \nstrong ridge is expected to develop across the western US with \ndownstream troughing across the eastern US.  Model solutions have \nbeen trending stronger with this wave and a blast of cold air may \ninfiltrate the Ohio Valley on/after 10/6 or so and linger for \nseveral days.  The pattern looks likely to relax a bit.  However, \nwe'll see a west Pacific typhoon (Surigae) recurve and merge into \nthe Pacific Jet as we cross into October.   This is likely to \nperturb the jet a bit and lead to a stronger west coast Ridge and \ndeeper eastern US trough with another surge of cool air arriving \naround the middle of October.  Longer range teleconnection patterns \ngoing into Mid-October feature a -AO/-NAO/+PNA pattern with the EPO \nflipping from positive to negative around mid-month.  This would \nsuggest that colder than normal conditions may develop/continue \nacross the Ohio Valley from Mid-October onward.",
